{
  "original_id": "primary-20260913-chen-ulc-failure",
  "archive_id": "BW2-PR20260913-chen_ulc_failure",
  "title": "Higher-index failure of ultra-log-concavity for Chen's recursion polynomials",
  "batch_label": "primary",
  "kind": "resolution-paper",
  "original_status": "proved",
  "scope": "For every integer m >= 4, let f_m(s)=((s+1)^m-(-1)^m)/(s+2) and h_m(s)=((s+1)^m+(-1)^m(s+1))/(s+2). For either P in {f_m,h_m}, write P(s)=sum_{j=0}^{m-1} a_j s^j. These are monic degree-(m-1) polynomials with nonnegative integer coefficients. For every integer j satisfying 4 <= j <= m-2, j(m-1-j)a_j^2 < (j+1)(m-j)a_{j-1}a_{j+1}. Moreover, for every m >= 4 the difference j(m-1-j)a_j^2-(j+1)(m-j)a_{j-1}a_{j+1} equals -m at j=m-2. Thus neither f_m nor h_m is ordinary ultra-log-concave, meaning that (a_j/binom(m-1,j)) is not log-concave, for any m >= 4. This includes both clauses of source.md and the m=4,5 cases with empty higher-index range.",
  "gate_basis": "Current released terminal, manifest, PDF, TeX freeze, snapshot files, provenance and four audits agree.",
